│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ aggressive_regularization_reduction_with_signal_features                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The core problem is that heavy L2/L1 regularization (reg_lambda=0.5, reg_alpha=0.05) is   │
│                          │ collapsing predictions toward the mean, producing pred_std_ratio~0.056 instead of the     │
│                          │ needed 0.15+. Prior runs show the best results when regularization is lighter (e.g.,      │
│                          │ reg_lambda=0.1, reg_alpha=0.0-0.01).                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Changes:                                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. **XGB_PARAMS**: Drastically reduce reg_lambda (0.5→0.15), eliminate reg_alpha          │
│                          │ (0.05→0.0), increase learning_rate (0.05→0.08), allow min_child_weight to go to 0,        │
│                          │ increase max_depth to 7 for flexibility, raise n_estimators to 1200 with aggressive early │
│                          │ stopping (40).                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **build_features()**: Add short-term mean-reversion and directional-bias features that │
│                          │ XGBoost can latch onto:                                                                   │
│                          │    - Short-term price extremes (5-bar and 10-bar percentile ranks) to capture             │
│                          │ mean-reversion setups                                                                     │
│                          │    - Intrabar momentum acceleration (ret_accel_3_12, ret_accel_6_24)                      │
│                          │    - Return entropy/skewness over short windows for tail-risk detection                   │
│                          │    - Close relative to recent range (close_rank_8) for quick reversals                    │
│                          │    - Simple price velocity (close - close_shift_4) as a leading signal                    │
│                          │    These are all properly shifted and past-only.                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **CLIP_PERCENTILE**: Increase from 98.0→99.0 to preserve tail signal while still       │
│                          │ clipping outliers.                                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected outcome: Higher pred_std_ratio (target 0.10+), better signal capture, and        │
│                          │ val_DA→0.53+.                                                                             │
