                        Patch Metadata - Gen 95/100 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/1                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ more_signal_less_collapse                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current model is still under-dispersed (`pred_std_ratio=0.11`) and the validation     │
│                          │ edge is only slightly above chance. The safest way to improve both directional signal and │
│                          │ output variance is to make the booster a bit more expressive while adding a few           │
│                          │ high-value, strictly past-looking features that capture short-horizon momentum, trend     │
│                          │ persistence, and regime shifts.                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I’m proposing:                                                                            │
│                          │ 1. Loosen the XGBoost regularization slightly and lower the learning rate with more       │
│                          │ estimators so the model can fit richer nonlinear structure without becoming too brittle.  │
│                          │ 2. Add a compact set of lagged / regime-aware features built from shifted series only:    │
│                          │    - short-horizon return stack on past closes                                            │
│                          │    - return trend and return acceleration                                                 │
│                          │    - rolling slope proxy for trend persistence                                            │
│                          │    - breakout position and range expansion                                                │
│                          │    - volume surprise and volume trend                                                     │
│                          │    - volatility compression / expansion                                                   │
│                          │    - optional orderbook imbalance change and funding momentum, shifted to remain          │
│                          │ past-only                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
